/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: IC-201 Securing Hybrid Cloud Infrastructure */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["IC-201"] = {
ov:"Cloud dissolves the traditional perimeter and shifts security to identity, configuration, and shared responsibility. This operational course builds hands-on cloud security: the shared responsibility model, least-privilege cloud IAM, network and data protection, posture management, and cloud-native monitoring and incident response. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"Cloud Service and Deployment Models",d:"IaaS, PaaS, SaaS and public, private, hybrid.",
obj:["Distinguish IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS by what the provider manages","Distinguish public, private, hybrid, and community clouds","Explain how the model shapes security responsibility"],
read:["Cloud computing comes in service models that differ by how much the provider manages. Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) provides virtualized compute, storage, and networking — the customer manages the OS, applications, and data on top. Platform as a Service (PaaS) provides a managed runtime and services — the customer manages only their application and data. Software as a Service (SaaS) delivers complete applications — the customer manages only their data and configuration. Each step up the stack hands more responsibility to the provider and leaves the customer less to secure, but the customer never fully escapes responsibility (module two makes this precise).",
"Deployment models describe where and for whom the cloud runs: public cloud (shared multi-tenant infrastructure from a provider), private cloud (dedicated to one organization), hybrid cloud (combining public and private/on-premises with integration between them), and community cloud (shared among organizations with common requirements, common in government). Most real environments are hybrid — some workloads on-premises, some in public cloud — which is why this course emphasizes securing across the boundary. The model chosen shapes the entire security approach: what you control, what you inherit, and where the trust boundaries fall."],
ex:"An agency runs a hybrid environment: legacy systems on-premises (private/IaaS-like), a customer-facing application on public PaaS, and email on SaaS. Each carries a different security responsibility split — the on-premises systems are fully theirs, the PaaS app shares responsibility with the provider, and for SaaS email they secure mainly configuration and data. Recognizing which model each workload uses is the first step to securing it correctly.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-145","The NIST definition of cloud computing — service and deployment models."],["CSA Security Guidance v4","Cloud security across service and deployment models."]],
kc:[
{q:"In Platform as a Service (PaaS), the customer typically manages:",o:["The physical hardware","Their application and data, while the provider manages the runtime and infrastructure","Nothing at all","The hypervisor"],a:1,x:"PaaS manages the platform/runtime; the customer is responsible for their application code and data on top of it."},
{q:"A hybrid cloud is:",o:["Two public clouds","A combination of public and private/on-premises infrastructure with integration between them","A private cloud only","SaaS email"],a:1,x:"Hybrid combines deployment models, which is why securing across the on-premises/cloud boundary is central."},
{q:"Moving up the stack from IaaS to SaaS, the customer's security responsibility:",o:["Increases","Decreases (the provider manages more), but never disappears entirely","Stays identical","Becomes zero"],a:1,x:"Higher-level services shift more responsibility to the provider, but the customer always retains data and configuration responsibility."}]},
{t:"The Shared Responsibility Model",d:"Who secures what — and the gap where breaches happen.",
obj:["Explain the shared responsibility model precisely","Identify which controls belong to provider vs. customer","Explain how misunderstanding the split causes breaches"],
read:["The shared responsibility model is the single most important concept in cloud security: the provider secures the cloud (physical infrastructure, hypervisor, managed services), and the customer secures what they put in the cloud (their data, access configuration, application security, and — in IaaS — the OS and network settings). The exact split shifts with the service model, but a constant holds: the customer is always responsible for their data and for configuring access to it correctly. This maps directly to the RMF control-inheritance concept from BC-105 — some controls are inherited from the provider, others remain the customer's.",
"The dangerous gap is the assumption error: customers assuming the provider covers a control the customer actually owns. The provider secures the storage service's infrastructure, but the customer configures whether a storage bucket is public or private — and the endless stream of exposed-bucket breaches comes from customers assuming security they never configured. Misconfiguration, not provider failure, is the leading cause of cloud breaches, precisely because the responsibility boundary is misunderstood. Getting this model right — knowing exactly which controls are yours — is the foundation on which every other cloud security practice rests, and it is why cloud posture management (module ten) exists to catch the customer-side mistakes."],
ex:"An organization stores sensitive data in cloud object storage and assumes 'the cloud is secure.' But making the bucket private was the customer's responsibility, not the provider's, and it was left public — exposing the data. The provider's infrastructure was never breached; the customer's configuration was wrong. This exact pattern, the misunderstood responsibility boundary, is the most common cloud breach, and understanding the model is the fix.",
refs:[["AWS/Azure/GCP Shared Responsibility Model documentation","The provider/customer control split per service model."],["Cloud Security Alliance, top cloud threats","Misconfiguration and the responsibility gap as leading breach causes."]],
kc:[
{q:"Under the shared responsibility model, the customer is always responsible for:",o:["The physical data center","Their data and the configuration of access to it","The hypervisor","The provider's network hardware"],a:1,x:"Regardless of service model, the customer owns their data and access configuration — the provider secures the underlying infrastructure."},
{q:"The leading cause of cloud breaches is:",o:["Provider infrastructure failures","Customer misconfiguration — assuming the provider covers a control the customer actually owns","Hypervisor exploits","Physical theft"],a:1,x:"The responsibility gap — customers not configuring controls they own, like bucket privacy — drives most cloud breaches."},
{q:"An exposed public storage bucket holding sensitive data is:",o:["The provider's fault","A customer misconfiguration — bucket access is the customer's responsibility","Impossible in cloud","A hypervisor bug"],a:1,x:"The provider secures the storage service; the customer configures access — a public bucket is a customer-side error."}]},
{t:"Cloud Identity and Access Management",d:"Identity as the cloud control plane.",
obj:["Explain why identity is the primary cloud perimeter","Describe cloud IAM constructs (users, roles, policies)","Explain federation and centralized cloud identity"],
read:["In cloud, identity is the perimeter — there is no network boundary to hide behind, so who can do what to which resources is the primary control. Cloud IAM systems (AWS IAM, Azure AD/Entra, GCP IAM) express access as policies attached to identities (users, groups) and roles (assumable sets of permissions), granting permissions to perform actions on resources. Understanding these constructs is essential because a single over-permissive policy can expose an entire cloud environment, and because cloud's programmatic nature means IAM misconfigurations are exploited at machine speed.",
"Cloud IAM should be centralized and federated rather than sprawling into per-service local accounts. Federating cloud access to the enterprise identity provider (the SSO of BC-104) means one authoritative identity source, centralized MFA and conditional access, and single-point deprovisioning — a departed employee loses cloud access when their central identity is disabled. Roles, especially, embody least privilege in cloud: rather than long-lived credentials, workloads and users assume roles that grant scoped, temporary permissions. This connects the identity architecture of BC-104 to the cloud: the same principles (least privilege, MFA, lifecycle, federation) apply, with even higher stakes because cloud identities control infrastructure directly."],
ex:"An organization federates its cloud access to its enterprise identity provider, so cloud logins use the same phishing-resistant MFA and conditional access as everything else, and disabling a departing employee's central identity removes their cloud access instantly. Workloads assume scoped roles rather than holding long-lived keys. The identity discipline of BC-104, applied to cloud, makes identity the strong perimeter that cloud requires.",
refs:[["AWS/Azure/GCP IAM documentation","Cloud identity constructs: users, roles, policies, federation."],["NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust","Identity as the control plane in perimeter-less environments."]],
kc:[
{q:"In cloud environments, the primary security perimeter is:",o:["The network firewall","Identity — who can perform which actions on which resources","The physical building","The hypervisor"],a:1,x:"Cloud has no traditional network boundary; IAM policy is the primary control over access to resources."},
{q:"Federating cloud access to the enterprise identity provider gives:",o:["More local accounts","One authoritative identity source with centralized MFA and single-point deprovisioning","Weaker security","No logging"],a:1,x:"Federation centralizes cloud identity, so MFA, conditional access, and disabling a departed user all apply from one place."},
{q:"Cloud roles (assumable, scoped, temporary permissions) embody which principle?",o:["Maximum access","Least privilege — scoped, temporary permissions instead of long-lived broad credentials","Shared accounts","No authentication"],a:1,x:"Roles grant just-enough, time-limited permissions, applying least privilege to cloud workloads and users."}]},
{t:"Least-Privilege Cloud Policies",d:"Scoping permissions and avoiding privilege sprawl.",
obj:["Explain least-privilege policy design in cloud","Identify over-permissive policy patterns","Explain policy analysis and permission right-sizing"],
read:["Cloud IAM policies grant permissions, and the pervasive failure is over-permissioning — granting broad access because it is easier than scoping precisely. Wildcard permissions (allow all actions on all resources), overly broad managed policies, and long-lived access keys with excessive rights are the cloud equivalent of the standing-privilege problems from BC-104, but with infrastructure-wide consequences: an over-permissive policy on a compromised identity can delete or exfiltrate an entire environment. Least-privilege cloud policy means granting exactly the actions on exactly the resources an identity needs, and no more.",
"Right-sizing permissions is an ongoing discipline. Cloud providers offer access analyzers that examine actual usage and flag unused permissions, and policy-analysis tools that detect over-broad grants, public exposure, and privilege-escalation paths (permissions that let an identity grant itself more permissions — a subtle and dangerous pattern). The practice is to start minimal and add as needed (rather than start broad and never trim), to prefer scoped roles over long-lived keys, to eliminate wildcard grants, and to periodically review and reduce permissions against actual usage. This is least privilege and access recertification (BC-104) applied to the cloud, where the blast radius of an over-permissive identity is the whole infrastructure."],
ex:"A security review runs the cloud access analyzer and finds a service account with a wildcard policy (all actions, all resources) used by an application that actually needs only read access to one storage bucket. The permissions are right-sized to exactly that, eliminating a catastrophic blast radius — a compromise of that identity would now expose one bucket's reads, not the entire environment. The tooling surfaced the over-permission that manual review missed.",
refs:[["AWS IAM Access Analyzer / Azure/GCP policy analysis","Detecting over-permissions and right-sizing access."],["NIST SP 800-53 AC-6 and cloud least-privilege guidance","Least privilege applied to cloud identities."]],
kc:[
{q:"A wildcard cloud IAM policy (all actions on all resources) is dangerous because:",o:["It is slow","A compromise of that identity can affect the entire environment — infrastructure-wide blast radius","It uses too much storage","It requires MFA"],a:1,x:"Over-broad grants give a compromised cloud identity infrastructure-wide power to delete or exfiltrate everything."},
{q:"A cloud privilege-escalation path is a policy that:",o:["Denies all access","Lets an identity grant itself additional permissions — a subtle, dangerous pattern","Encrypts data","Requires re-authentication"],a:1,x:"Permissions to modify IAM policies can let an identity escalate its own access; policy analyzers specifically flag these paths."},
{q:"Least-privilege cloud policy is best achieved by:",o:["Starting broad and never trimming","Starting minimal, adding as needed, and right-sizing against actual usage with access analyzers","Using wildcards for simplicity","Sharing one admin key"],a:1,x:"Minimal-by-default plus usage-based right-sizing keeps permissions scoped, unlike start-broad-and-forget."}]},
{t:"Cloud Network Security",d:"VPCs, security groups, and segmentation in the cloud.",
obj:["Describe cloud network constructs (VPCs, subnets, security groups)","Explain cloud segmentation and default-deny","Explain controlling ingress and egress"],
read:["Cloud provides software-defined networking: virtual private clouds (VPCs) or virtual networks partition your cloud space, subnets segment within them, and security groups and network ACLs act as virtual firewalls controlling traffic to and from resources. These are the cloud expression of the segmentation and boundary-protection principles from BC-102 — and because they are software-defined, they can be more granular and more consistently enforced than physical networks, but also more easily misconfigured. A security group left open to the internet (0.0.0.0/0) on a sensitive port is the cloud equivalent of an exposed service, and it is found by scanners within minutes.",
"The disciplines carry over with cloud specifics. Default-deny is the goal — security groups should permit only necessary traffic, and the common failure is overly permissive rules (allowing all inbound, or SSH/RDP from anywhere). Segmentation isolates workloads by function and sensitivity (a database subnet not reachable from the internet, only from the app tier). Egress control matters as much as ingress — restricting outbound traffic limits data exfiltration and C2. Private connectivity (VPC endpoints, private links) keeps traffic to cloud services off the public internet. The network layer complements identity: even with strong IAM, network segmentation contains what a compromised resource can reach."],
ex:"A review finds a database security group allowing inbound connections from 0.0.0.0/0 on the database port — the database is exposed to the entire internet. It is restricted to accept connections only from the application subnet, and made unreachable from the internet entirely. The cloud segmentation discipline (default-deny, tier isolation) closed an exposure that a scanner would have found and exploited within minutes.",
refs:[["AWS/Azure/GCP VPC and security group documentation","Cloud network constructs and controls."],["CIS cloud benchmarks, networking sections","Secure default-deny network configuration."]],
kc:[
{q:"A cloud security group allowing inbound from 0.0.0.0/0 on a sensitive port:",o:["Is secure by default","Exposes the resource to the entire internet — found by scanners within minutes","Improves performance","Is required"],a:1,x:"0.0.0.0/0 means any source; on a sensitive port it is an internet-exposed service, a cloud equivalent of an open door."},
{q:"Egress (outbound) control in cloud networking primarily:",o:["Speeds up downloads","Limits data exfiltration and command-and-control by restricting outbound traffic","Is unnecessary","Blocks all traffic"],a:1,x:"Restricting egress constrains what a compromised resource can send out — exfiltration and C2 — just as in on-premises networks."},
{q:"Cloud network segmentation complements strong IAM by:",o:["Replacing identity","Containing what a compromised resource can reach, even if identity controls are bypassed","Encrypting nothing","Removing the need for security groups"],a:1,x:"Defense in depth: network isolation limits blast radius at the network layer, backing up identity-layer controls."}]},
{t:"Hybrid Connectivity",d:"Securely linking cloud and on-premises.",
obj:["Describe secure hybrid connectivity options","Explain the transition-zone security considerations","Explain extending identity and monitoring across the boundary"],
read:["Hybrid environments must connect cloud and on-premises securely, and the connection is a critical trust boundary. Options include site-to-site VPN (encrypted tunnels over the internet — the IPsec of BC-102) and dedicated private connections (direct links that bypass the public internet entirely, offering higher performance and a smaller attack surface). Either way, the connection extends your network across a boundary, and the security question is what each side exposes to the other: a poorly-secured connection lets a compromise on one side reach the other, so the transition zone between them warrants firewalling and monitoring like any boundary.",
"The deeper challenge is consistency across the boundary. Identity should extend uniformly (the same federated identity and access controls apply whether a resource is on-premises or in cloud), monitoring should span both (a SIEM that sees cloud and on-premises logs together, so an attacker crossing the boundary is visible), and segmentation should not simply flatten when the environments connect (the cloud should not become an open extension of a flat on-premises network, or vice versa). Many hybrid breaches involve an attacker compromising the weaker environment and pivoting across the connection to the stronger one, which is why the boundary must be secured and monitored, not just established."],
ex:"An organization connects on-premises to cloud via a dedicated private link, but treats the cloud as fully trusted from on-premises, allowing broad access across the connection. An attacker who compromises an on-premises system pivots straight into cloud through the over-trusted link. Applying segmentation and least-privilege across the boundary — the cloud trusting on-premises only as much as necessary, with monitoring on the connection — would have contained the pivot.",
refs:[["AWS Direct Connect / Azure ExpressRoute / VPN documentation","Hybrid connectivity options and security."],["NIST SP 800-207 and cloud segmentation guidance","Extending Zero Trust across hybrid boundaries."]],
kc:[
{q:"A dedicated private connection (vs. site-to-site VPN over the internet) offers:",o:["No security benefit","Higher performance and a smaller attack surface by bypassing the public internet","Weaker encryption","No monitoring"],a:1,x:"Private links avoid the public internet, reducing exposure, though the trust boundary still requires firewalling and monitoring."},
{q:"Many hybrid breaches involve an attacker:",o:["Only staying on-premises","Compromising the weaker environment and pivoting across the connection to the stronger one","Attacking the provider's hardware","Guessing the ExpressRoute password"],a:1,x:"An over-trusted connection lets a compromise on one side reach the other; the boundary must be segmented and monitored."},
{q:"Consistency across the hybrid boundary means:",o:["Flattening both networks together","Extending identity, monitoring, and segmentation uniformly so neither side becomes an open extension of the other","Trusting cloud completely","Disabling logging in cloud"],a:1,x:"Uniform identity, monitoring, and segmentation prevent the boundary from becoming a soft pivot point between environments."}]},
{t:"Cloud Data Protection and Encryption",d:"Protecting data at rest and in transit with managed keys.",
obj:["Explain cloud encryption at rest and in transit","Describe key management services and key control","Explain data classification and protection in cloud"],
read:["Cloud data protection rests on encryption and key management. Encryption at rest is often available by default or with a click (encrypting stored data), and encryption in transit (TLS) protects data moving to and within the cloud. The consequential decisions are about keys: cloud Key Management Services (KMS) generate, store, and control encryption keys, and the customer chooses between provider-managed keys (convenient), customer-managed keys (the customer controls key policy, rotation, and can revoke access), and customer-supplied keys (highest control, most operational burden). Who controls the keys controls the data — revoking a key renders the data it protects inaccessible, which is powerful for both security and, if mismanaged, availability.",
"Key management is where cloud data protection succeeds or fails. Keys must have their own least-privilege access policies (who can use the key is as important as who can access the data), be rotated, and be protected — because a key with overly broad access undermines the encryption entirely. Beyond encryption, data protection in cloud includes classification (knowing what sensitive data lives where, often across many services), data loss prevention, and access logging on data stores. The recurring theme: encryption is necessary but its value depends entirely on key control, and the customer's responsibility (module two) squarely includes configuring encryption and managing keys correctly."],
ex:"An organization uses customer-managed keys in the cloud KMS for its sensitive data, with a tight key policy controlling which identities may use each key, automatic rotation, and full logging of key usage. When a compromised identity attempts to access encrypted data, it lacks key-use permission and fails. Because the customer controlled the keys with least-privilege policy, the encryption actually protected the data — unlike a broadly-accessible key that would have offered no real protection.",
refs:[["AWS KMS / Azure Key Vault / GCP KMS documentation","Cloud key management and encryption options."],["NIST SP 800-57","Key management best practices."]],
kc:[
{q:"In cloud encryption, who controls the keys:",o:["Is irrelevant","Effectively controls the data — revoking a key renders the protected data inaccessible","Is chosen by the provider only","Does not affect security"],a:1,x:"Key control is decisive; customer-managed keys let the organization control policy, rotation, and revocation of access to encrypted data."},
{q:"A key with overly broad access policy:",o:["Improves performance","Undermines the encryption entirely, since anyone who can use the key can access the data","Is required for rotation","Cannot be used"],a:1,x:"Encryption's value depends on key access control; a broadly-accessible key provides no real protection against a compromised identity."},
{q:"Encryption at rest in cloud is:",o:["The provider's sole responsibility","Often available but must be configured and its keys managed by the customer — part of the customer's responsibility","Never available","Automatic and needs no key management"],a:1,x:"Configuring encryption and managing keys is the customer's responsibility under the shared responsibility model."}]},
{t:"Object Storage Security",d:"Preventing the classic exposed-bucket breach.",
obj:["Explain object storage access controls","Identify misconfigurations that expose data","Describe defense-in-depth for storage"],
read:["Object storage (S3, Blob, Cloud Storage) is where the most notorious cloud breaches happen, because it is easy to store vast amounts of sensitive data and easy to misconfigure its access. Storage access is governed by multiple layers — bucket-level policies, object-level permissions, public-access settings, and access-control lists — and the classic breach is a bucket inadvertently made public, exposing everything in it to the entire internet. Because these breaches are so common and so damaging, providers now offer account-level public-access blocks that override individual bucket settings, which should be enabled as a safety net.",
"Defense in depth for storage layers several controls: block public access at the account level unless explicitly needed; enforce encryption; use bucket policies that grant least-privilege access to specific identities; enable access logging and monitoring (so unusual access is visible); and use posture management (module ten) to continuously detect any bucket that becomes public. Versioning and object-lock protect against deletion and ransomware. The lesson from module two is sharpest here: the provider secures the storage service's infrastructure flawlessly, but whether your bucket is public is entirely your configuration — and the endless exposed-bucket breaches are all customer-side misconfigurations, not provider failures."],
ex:"An organization enables account-level public-access blocking, so even if a developer accidentally sets a bucket to public, the account-level control overrides it and keeps it private. Posture management continuously scans for any exposed storage. When a misconfiguration is attempted, the safety net holds and the alert fires — layered controls prevented the single most common cloud breach that a lone bucket-policy check would have eventually missed.",
refs:[["AWS S3 / Azure Blob / GCP Storage security documentation","Storage access controls and public-access blocking."],["Cloud Security Alliance, storage misconfiguration guidance","Preventing exposed-bucket breaches."]],
kc:[
{q:"The classic notorious cloud breach involves:",o:["A hypervisor exploit","Object storage (a bucket) inadvertently made public, exposing its data to the internet","Physical theft","A DNS attack"],a:1,x:"Exposed-bucket breaches are the most common cloud data exposure, and they are customer misconfigurations, not provider failures."},
{q:"Account-level public-access blocking is valuable because it:",o:["Speeds up storage","Overrides individual bucket settings as a safety net, so an accidental public bucket stays private","Encrypts the data","Deletes old objects"],a:1,x:"The account-level control catches the accidental public-bucket mistake even if an individual setting is misconfigured."},
{q:"Defense in depth for object storage includes:",o:["Only encryption","Public-access blocking, least-privilege bucket policies, encryption, access logging, and continuous posture scanning","Making all buckets public","Disabling logging"],a:1,x:"Layered storage controls catch the misconfiguration that any single control would eventually miss — the recurring defense-in-depth theme."}]},
{t:"Cloud Logging and Monitoring",d:"Visibility into a programmable environment.",
obj:["Identify key cloud log sources","Explain monitoring the control plane vs. data plane","Explain centralizing and protecting cloud logs"],
read:["Cloud is programmable, so its most important logs record the control plane — every API call that creates, modifies, or deletes resources (AWS CloudTrail, Azure Activity Log, GCP Audit Logs). These reveal who did what to the infrastructure: an attacker creating a new admin identity, modifying a security group, or exfiltrating data via the API leaves a control-plane trail. Data-plane logs (access to the data itself, network flow logs, application logs) complement this. The control-plane log is the cloud analog of the audit logging from BC-103 — the record of security-relevant actions — and it must be enabled across all regions and accounts, because attackers exploit un-logged corners.",
"Cloud monitoring turns these logs into detection: alerting on suspicious API activity (a root account used, IAM policy changes, disabled logging, access from unusual locations), which is the cloud expression of the SIEM and detection disciplines. Logs must be centralized (aggregated across accounts to a central, monitored location) and protected from tampering — a common attacker move is to disable or delete cloud logging, so logs are sent to a separate, restricted account and their deletion or disabling is itself alerted on. The programmable nature of cloud makes both attack and detection fast: an attacker can pivot through an environment via API calls in seconds, but those calls are logged, so real-time monitoring of the control plane is the key defensive capability."],
ex:"Cloud monitoring alerts on a control-plane event: an IAM policy was modified to grant an identity broad permissions, from an unusual location, followed by an attempt to disable logging. Because control-plane logs were centralized to a protected account and monitored in real time, the attack is caught mid-sequence — the log-disabling attempt itself being a high-signal alert. The programmable attack was fast, but the logging captured it and the monitoring caught it.",
refs:[["AWS CloudTrail / Azure Monitor / GCP Audit Logs documentation","Cloud control-plane logging."],["CIS cloud benchmarks, logging and monitoring","Enabling, centralizing, and protecting cloud logs."]],
kc:[
{q:"The most important cloud logs for detecting infrastructure attacks record:",o:["Only application errors","The control plane — every API call that creates, modifies, or deletes resources","Only network bandwidth","Physical access"],a:1,x:"Control-plane logs reveal who did what to the infrastructure, the key trail for detecting cloud attacks."},
{q:"An attacker attempting to disable cloud logging should:",o:["Go undetected","Trigger a high-signal alert, since logs are centralized to a protected account and log-disabling is itself monitored","Succeed easily","Be ignored"],a:1,x:"Log-disabling is a common attacker move; protecting logs in a separate account and alerting on the attempt catches it."},
{q:"Real-time control-plane monitoring is critical in cloud because:",o:["Cloud is slow","The programmable environment lets attackers pivot via API calls in seconds — but those calls are logged","Logs are optional","Nothing happens quickly"],a:1,x:"Cloud attacks move at API speed; real-time monitoring of the logged control plane is the key to catching them."}]},
{t:"Cloud Security Posture Management",d:"Continuously detecting misconfiguration.",
obj:["Explain what CSPM does and why it is needed","Describe continuous compliance and drift detection in cloud","Explain automated remediation of misconfigurations"],
read:["Because misconfiguration is the leading cloud breach cause (module two) and cloud environments are large, dynamic, and multi-account, manual configuration review does not scale — Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) automates it. CSPM tools continuously scan cloud environments against security benchmarks (CIS cloud benchmarks, provider best practices) and policies, detecting misconfigurations — public buckets, over-permissive IAM, unencrypted data, open security groups, disabled logging — across all accounts and regions, and reporting or alerting on them. It is the continuous-compliance discipline from BC-103 applied to the ever-changing cloud.",
"CSPM's value is catching the misconfiguration before an attacker does. In a fast-moving cloud environment, a developer can create an exposed resource in seconds; CSPM detects it in near-real-time, and mature deployments automatically remediate high-risk misconfigurations (re-privatizing a public bucket, tightening an open security group) or at least alert immediately. This closes the gap between misconfiguration and exploitation that manual review leaves wide open. CSPM connects the shared-responsibility model to operational reality: it continuously verifies that the customer-side controls the organization is responsible for are actually configured correctly, across the whole environment, all the time."],
ex:"A developer, moving fast, creates a storage bucket and accidentally leaves it public. CSPM detects the exposure within minutes and — because it is configured for auto-remediation of public storage — re-privatizes it automatically while alerting the security team. The window between misconfiguration and potential exploitation was minutes, not the days or weeks a manual review cycle would have allowed. Continuous posture management caught what human review would have missed.",
refs:[["Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) vendor and CIS documentation","Continuous cloud configuration assessment and remediation."],["NIST SP 800-137 and cloud continuous monitoring","Continuous compliance applied to cloud."]],
kc:[
{q:"Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) exists because:",o:["Cloud has no misconfigurations","Misconfiguration is the leading breach cause and dynamic multi-account clouds outpace manual review","Providers require it","It replaces IAM"],a:1,x:"CSPM automates continuous configuration scanning at cloud scale, catching the misconfigurations manual review cannot keep up with."},
{q:"CSPM's core value is:",o:["Faster networking","Catching misconfigurations in near-real-time — before an attacker does — and often auto-remediating them","Encrypting data","Writing policy"],a:1,x:"CSPM closes the gap between misconfiguration and exploitation by detecting (and often fixing) exposures quickly."},
{q:"CSPM operationalizes the shared responsibility model by:",o:["Securing the provider's hardware","Continuously verifying the customer-side controls are correctly configured across the whole environment","Removing customer responsibility","Disabling logging"],a:1,x:"It continuously checks that the controls the customer is responsible for are actually configured right, everywhere, all the time."}]},
{t:"Container and Serverless Security",d:"Securing modern cloud-native workloads.",
obj:["Describe container security concerns in cloud","Explain serverless security considerations","Explain image scanning and workload identity"],
read:["Cloud-native workloads — containers and serverless functions — introduce their own security surface. Containers package applications with dependencies; their risks include vulnerable or malicious base images, embedded secrets, over-privileged containers, and a compromised container escaping to the host or moving laterally. Container security means scanning images for vulnerabilities and secrets before deployment (shift-left, per IC-203), using minimal hardened base images, running containers with least privilege (non-root, restricted capabilities), and controlling the registry and orchestration platform (Kubernetes) which is itself a high-value target with its own extensive security requirements.",
"Serverless functions remove server management but not security responsibility. Their concerns shift: the attack surface is the function's code, its dependencies, its permissions (an over-permissioned function is a serious risk, since it may be triggered by untrusted input), and its event sources. Because functions are ephemeral and event-driven, traditional host-based defenses do not apply, and security focuses on least-privilege function roles, dependency and code security, and input validation. Across both, workload identity — functions and containers authenticating as scoped identities rather than holding embedded secrets (the non-human identity discipline from BC-104) — is the modern best practice, eliminating the leaked-static-secret problem. These technologies deliver agility but demand that security move into the pipeline and the identity layer."],
ex:"A team scans every container image in its CI pipeline, rejecting any with critical vulnerabilities or embedded secrets, runs containers as non-root with least privilege, and gives serverless functions narrowly-scoped workload identities instead of embedded keys. When a dependency vulnerability is later disclosed, the image scan flags affected images immediately. Security built into the pipeline and identity layer secured the cloud-native workloads that host-based defenses could not.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-190","Application container security guide."],["CSA and provider serverless security guidance","Securing functions, permissions, and event sources."]],
kc:[
{q:"A primary container security practice is:",o:["Running everything as root","Scanning images for vulnerabilities and secrets before deployment and running least-privilege containers","Disabling image scanning","Embedding secrets in images"],a:1,x:"Image scanning (shift-left) plus least-privilege, non-root containers address the core container risks."},
{q:"Serverless function security focuses heavily on:",o:["Host hardening","Least-privilege function permissions, dependency/code security, and input validation — since functions are ephemeral and event-driven","Physical security","Disk encryption on the server"],a:1,x:"Ephemeral, event-triggered functions shift security to code, dependencies, permissions, and input, not traditional host defenses."},
{q:"Workload identity for containers and functions:",o:["Requires embedded static secrets","Lets workloads authenticate as scoped identities instead of holding secrets, eliminating the leaked-secret problem","Disables authentication","Is only for humans"],a:1,x:"Scoped workload identities (the non-human identity best practice) remove the static secrets that get leaked and abused."}]},
{t:"Cloud Compliance and FedRAMP",d:"Authorizing cloud for government use.",
obj:["Explain cloud compliance frameworks and FedRAMP","Describe inherited controls and the customer responsibility matrix","Explain impact levels for cloud"],
read:["Government use of commercial cloud requires assurance that the cloud meets security requirements, and FedRAMP (Federal Risk and Authorization Management Program) provides it: a standardized program that authorizes cloud services for federal use, based on the NIST controls (BC-106) at defined impact levels. A FedRAMP authorization means the cloud service provider has been assessed against a baseline and authorized, allowing agencies to use it without each independently reauthorizing the underlying platform — leveraging the inheritance and reciprocity concepts from BC-105 at scale. DoD adds Impact Levels (IL2 through IL6) for increasingly sensitive data.",
"The critical artifact for the customer is the Customer Responsibility Matrix, which makes the shared responsibility model concrete for a specific service: for each control, it states whether the provider fully satisfies it, the customer must, or it is shared. This is the inheritance mapping from BC-105 in operational form — it tells the customer exactly which controls they inherit from the FedRAMP-authorized provider and which they must implement and be assessed on themselves. Misreading this matrix (assuming inheritance where the customer is actually responsible) recreates the responsibility-gap breaches from module two, now with authorization consequences. Cloud compliance thus ties the whole course together: the shared responsibility model, control inheritance, and impact-based baselines applied to authorizing cloud for mission use."],
ex:"An agency deploys on a FedRAMP-authorized cloud platform and reads the Customer Responsibility Matrix carefully: it inherits the provider's physical, hypervisor, and platform controls but sees that data encryption, access configuration, and application security are customer responsibilities it must implement and be assessed on. By correctly mapping inherited versus customer controls, it builds a defensible authorization — avoiding the assumption gap that would leave customer-owned controls unimplemented.",
refs:[["FedRAMP program documentation and Customer Responsibility Matrix","Cloud authorization and the responsibility split."],["DoD Cloud Computing SRG (Impact Levels)","IL2-IL6 impact levels for cloud."]],
kc:[
{q:"A FedRAMP authorization allows agencies to:",o:["Skip all security","Use an assessed, authorized cloud service without each independently reauthorizing the underlying platform","Ignore the controls","Avoid the RMF"],a:1,x:"FedRAMP leverages inheritance and reciprocity so the authorized platform's controls can be inherited rather than re-assessed by each agency."},
{q:"The Customer Responsibility Matrix:",o:["Lists the provider's employees","States, per control, whether the provider, the customer, or both are responsible — the inheritance mapping in operational form","Is optional","Sets the price"],a:1,x:"It makes the shared responsibility model concrete per control, telling the customer exactly what they inherit versus must implement."},
{q:"Misreading the Customer Responsibility Matrix (assuming inheritance where the customer is responsible) causes:",o:["Faster authorization","The responsibility-gap problem — customer-owned controls left unimplemented, now with authorization consequences","Better security","Lower impact level"],a:1,x:"Assuming the provider covers a customer control recreates the module-two breach pattern, undermining the authorization."}]},
{t:"Cloud Incident Response",d:"Responding in a programmable, ephemeral environment.",
obj:["Explain how cloud changes incident response","Describe cloud-specific containment and evidence collection","Explain leveraging automation for cloud IR"],
read:["Incident response in cloud (BC-110's lifecycle) adapts to a programmable, ephemeral, API-driven environment. Detection relies on control-plane logs and cloud monitoring (module nine); containment uses cloud-native actions — isolating a compromised instance by changing its security group, revoking a compromised identity's sessions and keys, quarantining a resource — executed at API speed. The identity-layer containment from BC-110 is even more central in cloud, because compromised credentials and over-permissive roles are the primary attack path; disabling the identity and rotating its keys often matters more than isolating a host.",
"Cloud also changes evidence and recovery. Ephemeral resources (auto-scaling instances, containers) may disappear, so evidence collection must be fast and often automated (snapshotting a compromised instance's disk and memory via API before it is terminated). Recovery can leverage cloud's programmability — rebuilding from clean infrastructure-as-code rather than cleaning in place, which aligns with the rebuild-from-known-good principle from BC-110 and is often easier in cloud. Automation is the theme: because cloud attacks move at API speed and resources are ephemeral, cloud IR increasingly automates detection-to-containment (a compromised identity automatically quarantined on high-confidence detection), connecting to the SOAR discipline of IC-209. The lifecycle is the same; the mechanics are cloud-native and faster."],
ex:"Cloud monitoring detects a compromised identity making anomalous API calls. Automated response immediately revokes the identity's sessions and disables its keys (identity-layer containment), snapshots the affected instances for evidence before they auto-scale away, and alerts responders. Recovery rebuilds affected resources from clean infrastructure-as-code. The response used cloud-native, automated, API-speed actions — matching the speed of the attack in a way manual on-premises response could not.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2 and cloud IR guidance","Adapting the IR lifecycle to cloud."],["AWS/Azure/GCP incident response playbooks","Cloud-native containment, evidence, and recovery."]],
kc:[
{q:"Identity-layer containment is even more central in cloud IR because:",o:["Cloud has no identities","Compromised credentials and over-permissive roles are the primary cloud attack path — disabling the identity often matters most","Hosts cannot be isolated","Identities are irrelevant"],a:1,x:"Cloud attacks pivot via API using stolen credentials; revoking the identity and keys is frequently more important than isolating a host."},
{q:"Cloud evidence collection must often be fast and automated because:",o:["Logs are optional","Ephemeral resources (auto-scaling instances, containers) may disappear before manual collection","Cloud is slow","Evidence is unimportant"],a:1,x:"Ephemeral cloud resources vanish; automated snapshotting via API captures evidence before the resource is terminated."},
{q:"Cloud IR increasingly automates detection-to-containment because:",o:["Automation is trendy","Cloud attacks move at API speed and resources are ephemeral, so machine-speed response is needed","Humans are unnecessary","It reduces logging"],a:1,x:"API-speed attacks demand automated containment (auto-quarantining a compromised identity), connecting cloud IR to SOAR."}]},
{t:"Cloud Threats and Attack Techniques",d:"How adversaries actually attack cloud.",
obj:["Describe common cloud attack techniques","Explain how cloud attacks differ from on-premises","Map cloud attacks to the controls that stop them"],
read:["Cloud attacks follow recognizable patterns that map to the course's controls. Initial access commonly comes through leaked credentials or keys (from code repositories, per BC-104), phishing of cloud identities, or exploitation of exposed services. Once in, attackers enumerate permissions (what can this identity do?), escalate privileges through IAM misconfigurations (module four's escalation paths), pivot via the API and assumed roles, access data (exposed buckets, databases), exfiltrate, and establish persistence by creating new identities or access keys. The programmable, API-driven nature means these steps happen fast and leave a control-plane trail (module nine).",
"Cloud attacks differ from on-premises in key ways: identity and API abuse replace network exploitation as the primary vector, the control plane is a target in itself (creating backdoor identities is easier than dropping malware), and the blast radius of a single over-permissive credential can be an entire environment. Frameworks like MITRE ATT&CK for Cloud catalog these techniques (BC-108's framework applied to cloud). Mapping the attack chain to defenses shows how the course's controls interlock: leaked-credential initial access is blocked by workload identity and MFA; privilege escalation by least-privilege policy; data access by encryption and storage lockdown; and every step is caught by control-plane logging and CSPM. Understanding the attacker's cloud playbook is what makes the defensive layers purposeful rather than arbitrary."],
ex:"An attacker finds a leaked access key, enumerates its permissions, escalates through an IAM misconfiguration to broader access, and creates a backdoor admin identity for persistence — all via API in minutes. Mapping this to defenses: workload identity would have prevented the leaked static key, least-privilege policy would have blocked the escalation, and control-plane monitoring plus CSPM would have caught the new-identity creation. The attack chain reveals exactly which controls each step needed.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK for Cloud","Cataloged cloud adversary tactics and techniques."],["Cloud provider threat and abuse research","Real-world cloud attack chains."]],
kc:[
{q:"The primary initial-access vector for cloud attacks is often:",o:["Physical intrusion","Leaked credentials/keys, phishing of cloud identities, or exposed services","Hypervisor exploits","Cutting fiber cables"],a:1,x:"Cloud attacks typically start with credential/key compromise or exposed services, then pivot via the API — identity is the front door."},
{q:"Cloud attacks differ from on-premises in that:",o:["They are slower","Identity and API abuse replace network exploitation as the primary vector, and the control plane is itself a target","They use no credentials","They cannot escalate"],a:1,x:"In cloud, creating backdoor identities via API often replaces dropping malware; identity/API abuse is the core attack surface."},
{q:"An attacker creating a new backdoor admin identity for persistence is best caught by:",o:["Antivirus","Control-plane logging and CSPM detecting the anomalous identity creation","A host firewall","Disk encryption"],a:1,x:"Identity creation is a control-plane event; monitoring and posture management detect the backdoor that host-based tools would miss."}]},
{t:"Cloud Security Capstone",d:"Building a secure hybrid cloud environment end to end.",
obj:["Synthesize identity, network, data, posture, and monitoring into a secure design","Apply the shared responsibility model throughout","Verify a cloud environment against least-privilege and posture standards"],
read:["This capstone assembles the course into a secure hybrid cloud design. Start from the shared responsibility model — know exactly which controls are yours. Build on federated, least-privilege identity (modules three, four) as the primary perimeter, with roles not long-lived keys. Layer network segmentation and default-deny security groups (module five), secure the hybrid connection (module six), encrypt data with customer-controlled keys (module seven), lock down object storage with public-access blocking (module eight), and enable protected, centralized control-plane logging with real-time monitoring (module nine). Run CSPM continuously to catch misconfiguration (module ten), secure cloud-native workloads through the pipeline and workload identity (module eleven), and map compliance responsibilities correctly (module twelve).",
"The design principle is defense in depth adapted to cloud: identity, network, data, and posture layers back each other up, so that a single misconfiguration or compromised identity does not expose everything. A compromised identity is limited by least-privilege policy and network segmentation; a misconfigured bucket is caught by public-access blocking and CSPM; an API-based attack is logged and monitored. Each layer catches what another misses, and the whole is verified continuously rather than assumed. The graded deliverable — building a secure cloud environment with restricted security groups, centralized auditing, and locked-down object storage — is exactly this, and its quality is judged by whether the layers contain the failure of any one of them.",
"The capstone's lesson is that cloud security is configuration and identity discipline continuously verified, not a product you buy. The perimeter is gone; identity is the control plane; misconfiguration is the enemy; and the shared responsibility model means the customer's diligence — least privilege, correct configuration, continuous posture management — is what determines whether the cloud is secure. An organization that internalizes this builds cloud environments that are more defensible than the on-premises ones they replaced; one that assumes the cloud is secure by default builds the next exposed-bucket headline.",],
ex:"A team builds a hybrid cloud environment applying every layer: federated least-privilege identity, segmented networks, customer-managed encryption, public-access-blocked storage, protected centralized logging, and continuous CSPM. A penetration test compromises a low-privilege identity but finds it can reach almost nothing — least privilege and segmentation contain it, CSPM shows no exposed resources, and the activity is logged and alerted. No single layer was perfect, but the layered design contained the compromise — cloud defense in depth verified in practice.",
refs:[["CIS cloud benchmarks and CSA Security Guidance v4","End-to-end secure cloud architecture."],["NIST SP 800-207 and provider Well-Architected security pillars","Layered, identity-centric cloud security design."]],
kc:[
{q:"In a secure cloud design, the primary perimeter is:",o:["The network firewall","Federated, least-privilege identity — the control plane in a perimeter-less environment","The physical building","The hypervisor"],a:1,x:"Cloud has no traditional perimeter; identity, with least privilege and federation, is the primary control plane."},
{q:"A secure cloud environment is judged by whether:",o:["It uses the most services","The identity, network, data, and posture layers contain the failure of any single misconfiguration or compromised identity","It has no logging","It trusts the provider completely"],a:1,x:"Defense in depth adapted to cloud: no single failure exposes everything, verified continuously — the containment standard."},
{q:"The overarching lesson of cloud security is that it is:",o:["A product you buy","Configuration and identity discipline continuously verified, since misconfiguration is the enemy and identity the control plane","The provider's job entirely","Impossible to achieve"],a:1,x:"Cloud security is the customer's continuous diligence — least privilege, correct configuration, posture management — not a purchased product."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: Sensitive data in a cloud bucket is exposed to the internet. Investigation shows the provider's infrastructure was never breached. The cause is:",o:["A hypervisor exploit","Customer misconfiguration — bucket access is the customer's responsibility under shared responsibility","Provider negligence","A DNS attack"],a:1,x:"The provider secures the storage service; the customer configures access — a public bucket is a customer-side error, the top cloud breach pattern."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A service account has a wildcard IAM policy but only needs read access to one bucket. The risk is:",o:["Slower performance","Infrastructure-wide blast radius — a compromise of that identity could affect the entire environment","Too much logging","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Over-broad grants give a compromised cloud identity power over everything; right-sizing to least privilege caps the blast radius."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An attacker compromises an on-premises host and pivots straight into cloud through an over-trusted connection. The failure was:",o:["Using a VPN","Not extending segmentation and least privilege across the hybrid boundary","Encrypting the link","Monitoring the connection"],a:1,x:"An over-trusted connection lets a weaker-environment compromise pivot to the stronger one; the boundary must be segmented and monitored."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Encrypted cloud data is accessed by a compromised identity because the key's access policy was overly broad. The lesson is:",o:["Encryption is useless","Encryption's value depends on key access control — a broadly-accessible key provides no real protection","Use no encryption","Keys don't matter"],a:1,x:"Who can use the key controls the data; least-privilege key policy is essential or the encryption is defeated."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A developer accidentally makes a bucket public, but it stays private anyway. This is because the organization:",o:["Uses no cloud","Enabled account-level public-access blocking that overrides individual bucket settings","Deleted the bucket","Has no data"],a:1,x:"Account-level public-access blocking is the safety net that catches accidental public-bucket misconfigurations."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An attacker modifies an IAM policy and then tries to disable cloud logging. The attempt is caught because:",o:["Logging cannot be disabled","Control-plane logs were centralized to a protected account and log-disabling is itself alerted on","The attacker was slow","Cloud has no logs"],a:1,x:"Protecting logs in a separate account and alerting on disabling attempts catches this common attacker move mid-sequence."},
{q:"SCENARIO: In a large multi-account cloud, manual configuration review keeps missing exposed resources. The right solution is:",o:["Review harder","Cloud Security Posture Management — continuous automated scanning with alerting and auto-remediation","Stop using cloud","Ignore the exposures"],a:1,x:"CSPM automates configuration assessment at cloud scale and speed, catching what manual review cannot keep up with."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A serverless function is triggered by untrusted input and holds an over-permissive role. The primary risk is:",o:["Slow execution","The function's excessive permissions could be abused via the untrusted trigger — least-privilege function roles are essential","Too much memory","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Over-permissioned, input-triggered functions are a serious risk; serverless security centers on least-privilege roles and input validation."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An agency misreads a FedRAMP Customer Responsibility Matrix, assuming the provider handles data encryption. The result:",o:["Faster authorization","A responsibility gap — a customer-owned control left unimplemented, undermining the authorization","Better security","Lower cost with no risk"],a:1,x:"Assuming inheritance where the customer is responsible recreates the exposed-control breach pattern, now with authorization consequences."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A pen test compromises a low-privilege cloud identity but it can reach almost nothing. This demonstrates:",o:["Luck","Cloud defense in depth — least privilege and segmentation contained the compromise, with CSPM and logging backing up","A broken test","Weak security"],a:1,x:"Layered cloud controls (least privilege, segmentation, posture management, monitoring) contain a single compromised identity — the design goal."}
]};
